---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import Nav from '../../components/sections/Nav.astro'
import Footer from '../../components/sections/Footer.astro'
import PageHero from '../../components/marketing/PageHero.astro'
---

<Base
  title="Cloud & hosting — xNet"
  description="How hubs extend a local-first workspace, the choice between managed hosting and your own server, and the work toward simpler setup."
>
  <Nav />
  <main id="main-content">
    <PageHero
      eyebrow="Cloud & hosting"
      title="A wider reach. Your own ground."
      description="Your device holds the working copy. A hub helps your workspace stay reachable, backed up, and connected when your other devices are offline."
    >
      <div class="m-actions">
        <a class="m-button" href="/cloud/pricing">
          Explore hosting plans →
        </a>
        <a class="m-text-link" href="/docs/guides/server/">
          Run your own hub ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </PageHero>
    <section class="m-section">
      <div class="m-wrap m-grid-2">
        <div>
          <p class="m-eyebrow">A post office for your workspace</p>
          <h2 class="m-heading mt-6">
            The cloud helps.
            <br />
            <em>You keep the copy.</em>
          </h2>
        </div>
        <div class="m-copy">
          <p>
            Local-first doesn’t mean living on an island. A hub can relay changes and keep backups
            while your devices come and go. Core editing stays on your device.
          </p>
          <p>
            You can run the open-source hub yourself or choose managed hosting. Those choices have
            different trust boundaries: a managed operator runs the software that stores and indexes
            your content. Hosting is a service you choose, with responsibilities worth
            understanding.
          </p>
          <a class="m-text-link mt-7" href="/privacy">
            Read the privacy details →
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="m-section">
      <div class="m-wrap">
        <div class="m-grid-3">
          <article class="m-step">
            <span class="m-status now">Available foundations</span>
            <h3>Run a hub.</h3>
            <p>
              The hub software and deployment guides are available now. Self-hosting gives you
              control of the operator, and also the work of maintaining it.
            </p>
            <a href="/docs/guides/server/" class="m-text-link">
              Server guide ↗
            </a>
          </article>
          <article class="m-step">
            <span class="m-status">Hosting option</span>
            <h3>Choose an operator.</h3>
            <p>
              Managed plans package hosting and operations. Check the plan details and current
              service status before choosing the setup for your work.
            </p>
            <a href="/cloud/pricing" class="m-text-link">
              Plans and limits →
            </a>
          </article>
          <article class="m-step">
            <span class="m-status">Being improved</span>
            <h3>Make it feel simple.</h3>
            <p>
              Easy onboarding, recovery, and cross-device setup are part of the roadmap. The goal is
              a private connected workspace without a lesson in server administration.
            </p>
            <a href="/roadmap" class="m-text-link">
              The road ahead →
            </a>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="m-section">
      <div class="m-wrap m-banner">
        <p>
          <strong>The post office should never become the landlord.</strong> That idea runs through
          our essays on ownership, exit, and the open commons. Hosting should make the workspace
          more useful while leaving a real route to running it yourself.
        </p>
        <a class="m-text-link shrink-0" href="/blog/the-loom-you-can-read">
          Follow the thread ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section id="enterprise" class="m-section">
      <div class="m-wrap m-grid-2">
        <div>
          <p class="m-eyebrow">Enterprise hosting</p>
          <h2 class="m-heading mt-6">
            Talk through
            <br />
            <em>your requirements.</em>
          </h2>
        </div>
        <div class="m-copy">
          <p>
            For region-pinned hosting, an identity provider, or a custom support agreement, get in
            touch to discuss what your organisation needs and what we can support.
          </p>
          <a
            class="m-button secondary mt-6"
            href="mailto:hello@xnet.fyi?subject=xNet%20Cloud%20Enterprise"
          >
            Contact sales ↗
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
